WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:05.620
If you're above a certain age, you know that
"Miami Vice" was once the coolest show on

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:06.620
TV.

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:09.990
Whether you're old enough to remember it or
not, keep watching to find out what made it

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:10.990
so cool.

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:16.330
Miami Vice was unlike anything else on the
air when it premiered in 1984.

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:22.089
The series sprung from the mind of NBC Entertainment
president Brandon Tartikoff after being inspired

00:00:22.089 --> 00:00:23.359
by MTV.

00:00:23.359 --> 00:00:28.689
The most revolutionary thing on '80s television
at the time was MTV, the network which at

00:00:28.689 --> 00:00:32.110
the time aired music videos 24 hours a day.

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:36.640
Tartikoff wanted NBC to cash in on the craze,
and while brainstorming ideas one day, he

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:40.489
wrote down two simple words: "MTV cops."

00:00:40.489 --> 00:00:45.060
He sent along this tiny memo to Hill Street
Blues writer and producer Anthony Yerkovich.

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:50.199
Yerkovich immediately started to work on a
project that could fit Tartikoff's vague description.

00:00:50.199 --> 00:00:55.299
Yerkovich had gotten the idea for a movie
about police officers in Miami working vice

00:00:55.299 --> 00:00:57.960
-- aka, the beat for drugs and prostitution.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:03.379
He turned it into a double episode-length
pilot script initially titled "Gold Coast".

00:01:03.379 --> 00:01:07.610
Speaking to Time in 1985, Yerkovich had this
to say about the idea:

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:12.159
"Even when I was on Hill Street Blues, I was
collecting information on Miami [...] It seemed

00:01:12.159 --> 00:01:17.460
to be an interesting socioeconomic tidepool:
the incredible number of refugees from Central

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:23.656
America and Cuba, the already extensive Cuban-American
community, and on top of all that, the drug trade."

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.080
Don Johnson has scored some notable parts
since spending six years starring as Sonny

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:30.509
Crockett on "Miami Vice."

00:01:30.509 --> 00:01:35.970
He played the title character on the light-in-tone
cop drama "Nash Bridges" and later had a turn

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:40.220
as a police chief with dark secrets on HBO's
"Watchmen."

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:45.350
But he'll likely never move out of the large
shadow cast by his big "Miami Vice" gig, so

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:49.200
it's baffling to think that NBC almost went
another way.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.390
Brandon Tartikoff pushed hard to cast Larry
Wilcox as Sonny Crockett on Miami Vice.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:59.270
At the time, Wilcox was just wrapping up a
long run as a motorcycle-riding highway patrolman

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:00.270
on CHiPs.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:05.049
Wilcox auditioned and filmed some fight-based
test shots for "Miami Vice," only for them

00:02:05.049 --> 00:02:09.640
to cast Johnson instead, who was just coming
off of five unsold pilots.

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:14.780
At one point, the show's producers toyed with
casting two major movie stars of the time,

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:16.500
Jeff Bridges and Nick Nolte.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:22.780
Part of what made Miami Vice so different
from other police and crime shows of the 1980s

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:23.780
was its look.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:28.930
And by look, we mean the unique grooming style
and fashion-forward clothes of Don Johnson's

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:30.170
character, Sonny Crockett.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:35.480
"I mean, uh, you're not exactly up my alley,
style and personality wise.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:37.670
Heaven knows I'm no box of candy."

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:41.650
He wasn't clean-shaven, and he didn't rock
an era-appropriate mustache.

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:45.990
However, he did sport the fuzzy beginnings
of a beard, as if he hadn't had a chance to

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:47.380
shave in a while.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:49.720
Johnson talked to Rolling Stone about the
look, saying:

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:53.360
"The stubble was born out of the character,
because it was intimated that he had been

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:56.510
up partying with drug dealers for two or three
days at a time.

00:02:56.510 --> 00:03:00.780
That was sort of an unspoken thing, which
is why he was always unshaven and looked like

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:02.310
he slept in his clothes."

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:07.220
As for those clothes, countless guys co-opted
the Sonny Crockett 'look' -- a light-colored

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:11.400
blazer over a bright T-shirt, as well as shoes
with no socks.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.650
All of those choices were just practical.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:14.650
Johnson added:

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:18.000
"The rolled-up sleeves was a function of the
fact that I had to have a jacket to cover

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:19.000
the gun and the holster.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.650
I just stripped everything down to the bare
minimum.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:25.580
I didn't wear socks because it was too hot
to wear damn socks."

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:27.160
After all, they were in Miami.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:31.650
The popularity of Miami Vice peaked in its
second season.

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:36.930
For the year of 1985 to '86, it ranked #9
in the Nielsen ratings.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:41.850
NBC naturally ordered up a third season, but
star Don Johnson balked at his salary.

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:47.070
The $35,000 per episode he'd been making up
to that point was commensurate for an actor

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:51.350
on a new show, not the mega-star of an ultra-successful
hit.

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:56.971
According to the Associated Press, Johnson
wanted a raise to upwards of $100,000 per

00:03:56.971 --> 00:03:57.971
episode.

00:03:57.971 --> 00:04:02.600
He feuded with his bosses at Universal Television,
and refused to report to work until he got

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:04.310
what he wanted.

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:09.140
Executives called his bluff, filing a $10
million breach of contract lawsuit and leaking

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:12.920
the news that they were prepared to let Johnson
leave the series.

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:17.539
They even had two potential actor replacements
lined up: "St. Elsewhere" star Mark Harmon

00:04:17.539 --> 00:04:19.489
and the rising Treat Williams.

00:04:19.489 --> 00:04:24.699
Shortly after those aggressive actions, the
parties worked out a mutually agreeable arrangement.

00:04:24.699 --> 00:04:29.550
Johnson returned to the Miami Vice set as
a newly minted member of TV's highest paid

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:30.550
actors club.

00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:36.090
Show creator Anthony Yerkovich supervised
the pilot and first five episodes of Miami

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:39.699
Vice, then moved on to make movies for Universal
Pictures.

00:04:39.699 --> 00:04:44.590
This left the many day-to-day operations in
the hands of executive producer Michael Mann.

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:49.900
The show was so young and relatively undeveloped
that Mann could greatly dictate its direction,

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:53.740
particularly its visual style and pastel-heavy
color palette.

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:58.849
Mann sought to express Miami's uniquely bright
and eye-popping look on camera, inspired by

00:04:58.849 --> 00:05:03.960
memories of the city from a mid-'70s vacation
as well as a random trip to a paint store

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:06.270
one time when he was looking at color chips.

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:09.050
He shared this experience with the Los Angeles
Times:

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:14.289
"I was playing around with [the chips] and
I realized: three colors become thematic,

00:05:14.289 --> 00:05:17.789
two colors don't.Three colors, you can actually
start telling a chromatic story.

00:05:17.789 --> 00:05:19.850
You can create a mood with three colors."

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:24.169
As a result, Miami Vice employed a strict
hues policy.

00:05:24.169 --> 00:05:26.949
Miami Vice episode director Bobby Roth told
Time:

00:05:26.949 --> 00:05:31.650
"There are certain colors you are not allowed
to shoot, such as red and brown [...] Michael

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:33.620
knows how things are going to look on camera.

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:37.050
A lot of it is very basic stuff that has never
been applied to TV."

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:43.680
Miami Vice was, to use the language of then
and now, both cool and hot.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:49.389
It was so cool that its theme song for a time
became the hottest pop song in the land.

00:05:49.389 --> 00:05:54.039
The extended version of "Miami Vice Theme,"
written and performed by Jan Hammer, went

00:05:54.039 --> 00:05:58.610
to #1 on the Billboard Hot 100 in November
1985.

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:02.800
Even though Hammer never made it to the pop
chart again, his composition is still one

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:07.279
of only five TV theme songs to ever sit atop
the Top 40.

00:06:07.279 --> 00:06:11.569
As well as Hammer's theme song performed,
the first Miami Vice soundtrack album did

00:06:11.569 --> 00:06:12.569
even better.

00:06:12.569 --> 00:06:17.860
Released in 1985, the record included Hammer's
theme song and other bits of incidental music,

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:22.439
along with pop songs prominently featured
on the series, including Glenn Frey's "You

00:06:22.439 --> 00:06:25.669
Belong to the City" and Phil Collins' "In
the Air Tonight."

00:06:25.669 --> 00:06:31.430
It spent 11 weeks at #1 on the Billboard album
chart and sold four million copies, ranking

00:06:31.430 --> 00:06:36.029
it among the most successful TV tie-in albums
of all time.

00:06:37.029 --> 00:06:41.660
There's an old saying about how imitation
is the sincerest form of flattery, which means

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:46.240
the people who made Miami Vice must have been
very touched when the airwaves were flooded

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:47.279
with ripoffs.

00:06:47.279 --> 00:06:54.069
In 1985, a year after NBC premiered Miami
Vice, ABC answered with The Insiders, which

00:06:54.069 --> 00:06:58.819
it claimed offered "contemporary music" and
a "highly stylized visual approach."

00:06:58.819 --> 00:07:05.880
Nicholas Campbell and Stoney Jackson starred
as edgy crime reporter Nick Fox and an ex-con-turned-informant

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:07.180
named Mackey.

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:12.570
In true "Vice" style, the pilot episode contained
fancy cars, a visceral sequence depicting

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:16.210
a seedy crime, and almost everybody dressed
in hip clothes.

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:21.789
"The Insiders" was cancelled after 13 episodes,
and ABC's other Vice clone, "Hollywood Beat,"

00:07:21.789 --> 00:07:23.599
only lasted 14.

00:07:23.599 --> 00:07:28.669
Miami Vice also helped lead to one of the
most beloved and successful sitcoms of all

00:07:28.669 --> 00:07:29.669
time.

00:07:29.669 --> 00:07:33.650
Older actresses Doris Roberts and Selma Diamond
performed a bit during an event announcing

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:40.590
NBC's 1984-85 season and joked that they were
starring on a show called "Miami Nice."

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:45.349
That gave Tartikoff an idea -- a show about
retirement-age women all living together in

00:07:45.349 --> 00:07:46.729
Miami.

00:07:46.729 --> 00:07:52.520
That show, which hit the network in 1985 to
ratings and critical success, was none other

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:54.059
than "The Golden Girls."

00:07:55.129 --> 00:08:00.289
When "Miami Vice" debuted in 1984, the real
city of Miami wasn't as glamorous and flashy

00:08:00.289 --> 00:08:01.879
as the show made it seem.

00:08:01.879 --> 00:08:06.199
According to the Miami Herald, the city was
recovering from riots and struggling to house

00:08:06.199 --> 00:08:08.710
a large number of Haitian refugees.

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:13.849
Drug cartels were gaining power and influence,
and violent crime was dramatically rising.

00:08:13.849 --> 00:08:20.339
But Miami Vice made Miami's rundown, Art Deco
architectural style look cool and vital.

00:08:20.339 --> 00:08:24.099
That's a big part of what makes the city's
South Beach nightlife district so attractive

00:08:24.099 --> 00:08:25.159
to this day.

00:08:25.159 --> 00:08:30.190
Crews fixed up and painted over the dulled-with-time
exteriors of disused buildings, providing

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:34.120
Miami with a free makeover that was revitalizing
and restorative.

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:37.360
Don Johnson talked about this makeover to
Rolling Stone, saying:

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:42.500
"We painted the facades of virtually every
building up and down Collins Avenue and Ocean

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:46.250
Avenue to match the color palettes we had
for the show [...] The whole town kind of

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:49.520
reinvented itself in the image of a television
show."

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:54.750
All those reworked buildings allowed new businesses
in Miami to flourish, turning South Beach

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:59.640
into a tourist destination which initially
catered to big fans of Miami Vice.

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:04.960
Not only did Crockett and Tubbs dress cool,
they drove cool, too.

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:09.690
Since they frequently were going undercover,
and had access to sweet wheels that the MPD

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:14.970
seized from drug lords, the cops of "Miami
Vice" got to drive some enviable automobiles.

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:22.460
Detective Stan Switek drove a 1963 Ford Thunderbird,
Detective Tubbs had a 1963 Cadillac DeVille,

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:28.390
and Detective Crockett initially cruised with
a Ferrari 365 GTB/4 Daytona Spyder.

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:32.280
According to Popular Mechanics, Crockett's
car wasn't actually a Ferrari.

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:37.430
Instead, it was custom-made for the show,
assembled out of a 1980 Corvette with some

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:38.750
Ferrari parts.

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:43.580
This car garnered so much attention from fans
and car lovers that it rubbed executives at

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:45.260
Ferrari the wrong way.

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:50.460
Ferrari North America sought to divert attention
away from a car that wasn't commercially available,

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:55.870
so they provided two brand new Ferrari Testarossas
to the Miami Vice production.

00:09:55.870 --> 00:10:00.250
The Testarossas arrived painted black, but
when the cars didn't look good in some of

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:04.180
the show's many nighttime scenes, the crew
threw on some white paint.

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:10.690
What did all those things that made Miami
Vice so fresh and original actually cost?

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:12.160
They cost a lot.

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:17.840
In the first season of the show, producers
spent about $1.3 million per episode.

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:22.500
That's 30 percent more than what other police
series spent for an installment at the time.

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:29.210
When adjusted to today's current dollar value,
Miami Vice cost $3.1 million an episode -- and

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:31.520
that's before Don Johnson's big pay raise.

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:34.890
A substantial segment of the budget each week
went to music.

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:42.180
Early in its run, Miami Vice soundtrack supervisors
spent about $10,000 each week to license pre-existing,

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:47.560
well-known pop and rock songs by the likes
of U2, Tina Turner, Phil Collins, and Roxy

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:48.560
Music.

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:53.440
That costs a lot more than just hiring studio
musicians to make a generic, soft-rock score.

00:10:54.540 --> 00:11:02.590
Miami Vice faded into the collective turquoise-and-pink-tinted
memory in 1989 after falling to #64 in the

00:11:02.590 --> 00:11:07.970
annual Nielsen ratings, no longer the white-hot
show it was just a few years earlier.

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:13.680
By the mid-2000s, the franchise's good name
had switched from passé to nostalgia, and

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:18.780
series executive producer Michael Mann had
evolved into an accomplished and lauded filmmaker.

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:23.950
All of this, however, made for the perfect
climate for a Miami Vice revival on the big

00:11:23.950 --> 00:11:24.950
screen.

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:29.700
According to Kinetoscope, the film's production
was anything but perfect, going over budget

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:31.760
and enduring filming delays.

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:36.630
Star Jamie Foxx grew so disenchanted that
he left the film during a location shoot in

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:41.080
the Dominican Republic and refused to come
back for any overseas work.

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:45.790
This forced Mann to rewrite the ending and
abandon plans to shoot in Paraguay.

00:11:45.790 --> 00:11:50.140
Foxx and Mann really didn't get along, but
that wasn't an isolated situation.

00:11:50.140 --> 00:11:51.680
According to a crew member:

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:55.410
"Michael dressed down everybody and humiliated
everybody."

00:11:55.410 --> 00:12:01.550
Audiences, likely used to campy, ironic movie
versions of era-definitive TV shows, seemingly

00:12:01.550 --> 00:12:06.000
weren't ready for a gritty, dark, R-rated
drug trafficking thriller.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:09.110
Critics weren't either, and reviewed it tepidly
at the time.

00:12:09.110 --> 00:12:15.670
The 2006 film earned a middling $63 million
at the domestic box office, less than half

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:19.160
of the film's enormous $135 million budget.

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:26.410
If filmmakers or producers had any plans to
bring back Miami Vice in the wake of the 2006

00:12:26.410 --> 00:12:31.740
film version, the movie's lackluster performance
likely buried those plans in the ground.

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:37.470
But 2006 is a millennium ago to Hollywood,
and in the 2010s, talks started happening

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:41.500
on giving Miami Vice another, high-profile
shot.

00:12:41.500 --> 00:12:46.990
In 2014, Universal Pictures actively accepted
pitches from screenwriters who wanted to take

00:12:46.990 --> 00:12:51.310
a shot at launching a new franchise of Crockett
and Tubbs-headlined adventures.

00:12:51.310 --> 00:12:56.630
Those films unfortunately never came, but
later that year, original series star Don

00:12:56.630 --> 00:13:01.810
Johnson told Rolling Stone that there was,
quote, "talk recently about doing new episodes"

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:06.181
and that he had a few ideas on how to pass
the torch, narratively speaking, to a new

00:13:06.181 --> 00:13:08.300
generation of vice cops.

00:13:08.300 --> 00:13:14.360
In 2017, nearly three years later after those
negotiations allegedly took place, Variety

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:19.750
reported that movie star Vin Diesel and producer
Chris Morgan were hard at work on a new Miami

00:13:19.750 --> 00:13:21.750
Vice for NBC.

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:27.200
TV writer Peter Macmanus, who'd penned episodes
of The Mist and Satisfaction, was hired to

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:32.620
write the pilot script, but that new take
on "Miami Vice" never showed up, either.

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:35.520
